From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: net: wwan: mhi_wwan_mbim: run XDP natively on de-aggregated datagrams

This replaces the generic XDP hook 891 added with a native one: the program
runs on an xdp_buff over a bare frag, and an skb is built only if the verdict
is XDP_PASS.

The reason this is a contained change rather than a receive-path rewrite is
that the hard part was already done, in upstream, years ago. MBIM aggregation
delivers one NTB holding many datagrams, and mhi_mbim_rx() has always copied
each datagram out into its own allocation:

	skbn = netdev_alloc_skb(link->ndev, dgram_len);
	skb_put(skbn, dgram_len);
	skb_copy_bits(skb, dgram_offset, skbn->data, dgram_len);

No clone, no shared page, no per-packet refcount. The buffer is exclusively
owned from the moment it exists. 891 then added XDP_PACKET_HEADROOM to that
allocation. So every ingredient xdp_prepare_buff() wants was already present,
and 256 bytes of headroom clears the 40-byte sizeof(struct xdp_frame) floor
that xdp_convert_buff_to_frame() needs for XDP_REDIRECT. The only thing
separating this from native XDP was that the buffer got wrapped in an skb
before the program ran instead of after.

So the copy is unchanged -- same source, same length, same cost -- into a
netdev_alloc_frag() instead of a netdev_alloc_skb(), and build_skb() happens
on the far side of the verdict.

What that buys. XDP_DROP no longer allocates an skb at all, where the generic
path allocated one, ran the program on it, and freed it. XDP_REDIRECT can now
reach a cpumap, which is the reason to want any of this: the datagram loop
runs on one CPU, the MHI DL tasklet, and cpumap redirect is the mechanism for
moving per-packet work off it.

Native is also more correct here than generic, which is worth saying because
it is the opposite of the usual trade. bpf_prog_run_generic_xdp() reads bytes
0..5 and 12..13 of the packet as an Ethernet destination and ethertype and
acts on what it finds (dev.c:5093-5134). On a raw-IP link those offsets are
inside the IP header, and 891 carries a long comment and three stores to
repair the damage afterwards. Running the program on an xdp_buff never enters
that code, so the entire class of misparse is absent rather than corrected.

Details that are load-bearing rather than stylistic:

  * frame_sz must be the whole allocation including the skb_shared_info
    tailroom. xdp_data_hard_end() subtracts exactly that (include/net/xdp.h:147)
    and build_skb() reuses the same area, so understating it lets
    bpf_xdp_adjust_tail()'s memset run past the end of the buffer.
    netdev_alloc_skb() adds this room invisibly, which is why the non-XDP path
    never had to say so and why this is easy to get wrong.
  * The skb is sized from xdp.data and xdp.data_end, not from the original
    length, because bpf_xdp_adjust_head() and bpf_xdp_adjust_tail() may have
    moved either. That is what the headroom exists for.
  * bpf_net_ctx_set() is taken once for the whole NTB rather than per
    datagram, so redirects batch into a single xdp_do_flush(). It returns NULL
    if an outer caller already owns the context and bpf_net_ctx_clear(NULL) is
    a no-op (include/linux/filter.h:774, :786), so the pair is safe to take
    unconditionally, and both exits from mhi_mbim_rx() flush.
  * Context is the MHI DL tasklet: softirq with BH disabled, so the per-CPU
    redirect ring is safe and the tasklet cannot migrate between the program
    run and the flush. rcu_read_lock() is already held across the loop.
  * xdp_rxq_info is registered in ndo_init and unregistered in ndo_uninit,
    pairing with gro_cells exactly as 890 set up, so its lifetime is the
    netdev's registered lifetime and not the carrier state. napi_id is 0
    because this loop is not a NAPI poll.

Two honest limitations.

XDP_TX is not zero-copy. There is no ndo_xdp_xmit on this driver and
generic_xdp_tx() is not exported to modules, so the frame is wrapped in an skb
and re-enters the ordinary transmit path. That costs a qdisc traversal, and it
is done this way rather than dropped so the action set 891 supported is not
regressed. NETDEV_XDP_ACT_NDO_XMIT is deliberately not advertised: this device
can be a redirect source but not a redirect target.

Tail growth is bounded by the allocator's alignment rather than forbidden.
truesize is SKB_DATA_ALIGN(XDP_PACKET_HEADROOM + len) plus
SKB_DATA_ALIGN(sizeof(struct skb_shared_info)), so xdp_data_hard_end() lands
SKB_DATA_ALIGN(256 + len) - (256 + len) bytes past the datagram. On arm64
SMP_CACHE_BYTES is 64 and XDP_PACKET_HEADROOM is a multiple of it, so that is
(64 - len % 64) % 64: between 0 and 63 bytes, decided by the datagram length
alone. bpf_xdp_adjust_tail() growing by more than that returns -EINVAL;
growing by less may succeed. A program that grows the tail by a small amount
therefore works for some packet lengths and not others, which is the hazard
worth stating -- not an absence of slack. Reserving a guaranteed amount is a
per-packet memory cost that should be paid on evidence rather than by
default.

Depends on 873 for the payoff. Without it, __xdp_build_skb_from_frame() runs
eth_type_trans() over the IP header when cpumap rebuilds the skb and
ip_forward() drops every redirected packet -- the same Ethernet assumption,
one layer further out. 873 is core net and could be sent on its own; this
patch is what makes it reachable.

One piece of context for anyone sending this upstream. On 2026-08-14 Jakub
Kicinski refused a nearby patch with "There is no native XDP on any non-ether
device, making generic xdp work on those is silly." The first half of that is
a factual claim, and this patch is a measured counter-example to it: a
non-Ethernet device running native XDP, on the grounds that the buffer
discipline was already in the driver and only the verdict handling was
missing. Alexander Lobakin, in the same thread, listed cpumap Rx and
ndo_xdp_xmit as what would break -- he was right on both, and they are 873 and
the XDP_TX limitation above, handled rather than discovered late.

Evidence: E2. Every API contract above was read at v6.12.103 and every
statement about this driver was read against the tree with 890, 891 and 892
applied. Applies at --fuzz=0 with 870 through 892 in place, and shares a file
with 890, 891 and 892 only -- all three touch mhi_wwan_mbim.c, and this must
come after them.

Not built, not flashed, not measured. The verification plan is in
x3000/docs/native-xdp-wwan-design.md: confirm the path is native rather than
generic, measure XDP_DROP cost against the generic path, exercise XDP_PASS
with a head adjustment, redirect into a cpumap with and without 873, and
confirm a tail-growing program gets -EINVAL instead of scribbling.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build.

